  <p>
    Every figure below was checked against a primary source, and where we could not reach one, this
    page says so. If you find something wrong, <a href="/contact">tell us</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The law this is built on</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Income-tax Act, 2025</strong> replaced the Income-tax Act, 1961 on 1 April 2026, and
    the Income-tax Rules 2026 replaced the 1962 Rules. So FY 2026-27 is legally{' '}
    <strong>Tax Year 2026-27</strong>; the term &ldquo;assessment year&rdquo; no longer exists.
    Section numbers changed too, which is why this site cites section 156 rather than 87A, and
    section 202 rather than 115BAC.
  </p>
  <p>The Finance Act 2026 left the slabs, the standard deduction and the rebate unchanged.</p>

  <h2>Income tax</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>New regime slabs (s.202(1)):</strong> nil to &#8377;4 lakh, then 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%,
      25% and 30% in &#8377;4 lakh steps to &#8377;24 lakh.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Old regime slabs:</strong> nil to &#8377;2.5 lakh, 5% to &#8377;5 lakh, 20% to
      &#8377;10 lakh, 30% above. Resident senior citizens start at &#8377;3 lakh and super seniors
      at &#8377;5 lakh.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Standard deduction (s.19):</strong> &#8377;75,000 new, &#8377;50,000 old, never more
      than your salary.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rebate (s.156):</strong> &#8377;60,000 up to &#8377;12 lakh in the new regime, with
      marginal relief above it; &#8377;12,500 up to &#8377;5 lakh in the old regime, with no relief
      above it. Residents only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Surcharge:</strong> 10% above &#8377;50 lakh, 15% above &#8377;1 crore, 25% above
      &#8377;2 crore, and 37% above &#8377;5 crore in the old regime only. The new regime is capped
      at 25%.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cess:</strong> 4% on tax plus surcharge, after both marginal reliefs.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Marginal relief is applied as a real cap, not a flat percentage. At every threshold, tax plus
    surcharge is limited to the tax at the threshold plus the income above it. The 4% cess is then
    charged on that capped amount, so inside the relief band each extra rupee costs ₹1.04 and
    take-home dips very slightly until the band ends. <a href="/marginal-relief">Worked example</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Labour Code wage rule</h2>
  <p>
    Wages under the Code on Wages s.2(y) and the Code on Social Security s.2(88) are basic pay,
    dearness allowance and retaining allowance. If the excluded payments &mdash; HRA, conveyance,
    travel concessions, employer PF and similar &mdash; exceed half of your total remuneration, the
    excess is added back into wages. That figure is then the base for PF, ESI and gratuity.
  </p>
  <p>Two points in that rule are genuinely ambiguous, so here is exactly what we do:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What counts as total remuneration.</strong> We follow the Ministry of Labour&rsquo;s
      Additional FAQs of 16 March 2026: employer PF, pension contributions and statutory bonus are
      included; gratuity, ESI and other retirement benefits are not. An earlier FAQ of 30 December
      2025 put gratuity inside the total in its worked example, and the two conflict. We follow the
      later and more specific answer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Special allowance.</strong> It is named in neither the included nor the excluded list.
      The Ministry treats every payment other than basic, DA and retaining allowance as subject to
      the 50% test, so that is our default. The statute can also be read as making such allowances
      wages in full, which raises gratuity and can change ESI; advanced mode has a switch for that
      reading.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is <strong>no legal floor</strong> requiring Basic to be half of CTC, despite how often
    that is written. The rule changes the calculation, not your payslip.
  </p>

  <h2>Provident fund, ESI and gratuity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>PF:</strong> 12% employee and 12% employer under the EPF Scheme 2026, on Labour Code
      wages. The statutory wage ceiling is &#8377;15,000 a month, re-notified unchanged in May 2026.
      Most employers cap contributions there (&#8377;1,800 a month); some contribute on full wages.
      That is a switch, not an assumption. Of the employer&rsquo;s 12%, 8.33% of wages up to the
      ceiling goes to the pension scheme.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ESI:</strong> 0.75% employee and 3.25% employer, when wages do not exceed
      &#8377;21,000 a month (&#8377;25,000 for a person with a disability). Since 21 November 2025
      that ceiling is tested on Labour Code wages rather than gross pay, which can bring in
      employees whose gross is above the ceiling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gratuity:</strong> 15/26 of a month&rsquo;s wages for each completed year, with a part
      year above six months counted as a full year. Five years of service are needed, or one year
      for a fixed-term employee engaged directly, paid pro rata. Capped at &#8377;20 lakh.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>HRA</h2>
  <p>
    The exemption is the least of the HRA you receive, rent paid minus 10% of Basic, and 50% or 40%
    of Basic. From this year, Rule 279 of the Income-tax Rules 2026 puts{' '}
    <strong>eight cities</strong> at 50%: Mumbai, Kolkata, Delhi, Chennai, Hyderabad, Pune,
    Ahmedabad and Bengaluru. Sites still using the old four-metro list understate the exemption for
    Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Pune and Ahmedabad. HRA is not available in the new regime.
  </p>

  <h2>Professional tax</h2>
  <p>
    This is state law, capped at &#8377;2,500 a year by Article 276(2), and it is where most
    calculators are wrong. We compute it month by month:
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>State</th>
          <th>What we apply</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Maharashtra</td>
          <td>
            Men nil to &#8377;7,500, &#8377;175 to &#8377;10,000, then &#8377;200 with &#8377;300 in
            February. Women nil to &#8377;25,000, then the same. Exempt at 65.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Karnataka</td>
          <td>
            Nil below &#8377;25,000, then &#8377;200 with &#8377;300 in February. Exempt at 60.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Telangana and Andhra Pradesh</td>
          <td>
            Nil to &#8377;15,000, &#8377;150 to &#8377;20,000, then &#8377;200. No February change.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>West Bengal</td>
          <td>
            Old slabs to 30 September 2026, new slabs from 1 October 2026 (nil below &#8377;20,000,
            then &#8377;100, &#8377;140, &#8377;170 and &#8377;208).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Punjab</td>
          <td>
            &#8377;200 a month as State Development Tax, for employees liable to income tax. Often
            wrongly listed as a zero-tax state.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Delhi, Haryana, UP, Rajasthan, Uttarakhand, Himachal, Goa, J&amp;K and others</td>
          <td>No professional tax.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Every other levying state</td>
          <td>
            Shown as zero with a warning, plus a field to enter your payslip figure. We do not
            guess.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Professional tax is deductible in the old regime only.</p>

  <h2>What we assume</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You are a resident individual with salary income only, unless you say otherwise.</li>
    <li>Your salary is the same every month, with no mid-year joining, increment or job change.</li>
    <li>Variable pay is shown as if fully earned, and its tax is spread across the year.</li>
    <li>
      Where employer contributions to PF and NPS exceed &#8377;7.5 lakh a year, we tax the excess
      attributable to PF as a perquisite. Employer NPS is already inside taxable salary, so this
      avoids taxing the same rupees twice. The interaction of the two rules is not settled in
      published guidance.
    </li>
    <li>
      ESI is shown as a steady state for the year. In reality, someone who crosses the ceiling
      mid-period stays covered until the contribution period ends in September or March.
    </li>
    <li>Employer insurance premiums, ESOPs and reimbursements are not modelled.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we could not verify</h2>
  <p>These are open, and we would rather say so than paper over them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>West Bengal&rsquo;s new slabs.</strong> Two independent compliance advisories agree on
      the table and the 1 October 2026 date but cite different notification numbers, and no West
      Bengal gazette would load for us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Punjab&rsquo;s income threshold.</strong> The state&rsquo;s clarification is a scanned
      image we could not read, and its Act still refers to the repealed 1961 Act. We apply the levy
      above the basic exemption limit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Andhra Pradesh.</strong> Its slabs match Telangana in every compliance source, but the
      state portal would not serve its own schedule page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Slabs for the other levying states</strong>, including Tamil Nadu and Kerala, which
      charge half-yearly through local bodies rather than on a state monthly schedule.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When this was checked</h2>
  <p>
    Statutory rates were last verified on {SITE.ratesVerifiedOn} against the Income-tax Act 2025 and
    Rules 2026, the Finance Act 2026, Ministry of Labour FAQs, EPFO and ESIC notifications, and
    state professional tax Acts. They must be checked again after Union Budget 2027 before being
    used for the next year.
  </p>
